0,Tommo So - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tommo_So,"Tommo So is a language spoken in the eastern part of Mali's Mopti Region. It is placed under the Dogon language family, a subfamily of the Niger-Congo language family.","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
1,Dogon Language Family,"https://theswissbay.ch/pdf/Books/Linguistics/Mega+linguistics+pack/African/Niger-Congo/Dogon/Tommo+So,+A+Grammar+of+(McPherson).pdf","The Dogon language family is made up of around 20 languages, and it is currently thought to constitute its own branch of the Niger-Congo phylum (Blench XXX).","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
2,"Dogon, Tommo So Language (DTO)... | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/dto/,Tommo So Dogon is a stable indigenous language of Mali. It belongs to the Niger-Congo language family.,"Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
3,Dogon Phylogenetics,https://hal.science/hal-04577176v1/file/ZUK2024_Dogon_Phylogeny_HAL.pdf,"Are they Niger-Congo? • If so, where do they fit into the family tree? • Vestigial morphology.• Tommo So, spoken by some 40-60 000 speakers is the second largest Dogon language.","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
4,Glottolog 5.3 - Dogon,https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/dogo1299,"Tommo So Dogon.Dogon has not yet been shown to contain systematic sound correspondences, noun class systems or verbal extensions to Niger-Congo in some form Tom Güldemann 2018: 192-193 , 232-234, 353-358.","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
5,Dogon languages,https://grokipedia.com/page/Dogon_languages,"Southeastern plains (Tengu-Togo). Tommo So Dogon. ddd. Central escarpment.Core Niger-Congo retentions are sparse, as Dogon diverges lexically from other branches, with only limited shared roots in basic vocabulary.","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
6,Tone-tune association in Tommo So (Dogon) folk songs | Request PDF,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/323841619_Tone-tune_association_in_Tommo_So_Dogon_folk_songs,"... through morphological processes-show different patterns in text-setting in Med umba. For example, McPherson and Ryan (2018) show that melodic text-setting requirements are more stringent in Tommo So for lexical tone vs. grammatical tone.","Dogon, Tommo So",Niger-Congo
